WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.296
Welcome to Part 1 of the
deeper dive implementation

2
00:00:03.296 --> 00:00:05.938
of attribute-driven migration to Entra ID.

3
00:00:05.938 --> 00:00:08.400
The technical deep dive
explained the model.

4
00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:13.278
This series goes below it, to the work
an engineer actually runs: the commands,

5
00:00:13.278 --> 00:00:17.381
the attribute slots, the checks
and what their output looks like,

6
00:00:17.381 --> 00:00:19.162
and how to back each step out.

7
00:00:19.162 --> 00:00:23.663
It follows the implementation guide's ten
gates and the project plan behind them.

8
00:00:23.663 --> 00:00:28.596
Part 1 takes Gates 0 to 3: reading
the organizational unit (OU) tree,

9
00:00:28.596 --> 00:00:34.925
fixing the schema and slots, proving the
attributes, and mapping them through sync.

10
00:00:34.925 --> 00:00:37.662
Before that, we'll measure
how things are done today.

11
00:00:37.662 --> 00:00:41.706
Design guidance is labeled, and
the example code is illustrative,

12
00:00:41.706 --> 00:00:45.200
tested against fixtures
rather than a live tenant.

13
00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:50.988
Here are the four gates on one page.

14
00:00:50.988 --> 00:00:54.306
Gate 0 is done when the
tree's encoding is in Git,

15
00:00:54.306 --> 00:00:57.625
split by track, with a
decision for every group.

16
00:00:57.625 --> 00:01:00.400
It's read-only, so there's
nothing to roll back.

17
00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:03.646
Gate 1 is done when every
attribute that drives

18
00:01:03.646 --> 00:01:06.725
access has one store,
one writer and one owner.

19
00:01:06.725 --> 00:01:09.513
A slot can change only
until a rule reads it.

20
00:01:09.513 --> 00:01:13.208
Gate 2 is done when the attributes
are proven on the pilot,

21
00:01:13.208 --> 00:01:16.825
the other writers are gone,
and logs are leaving Entra ID.

22
00:01:16.825 --> 00:01:22.625
Gate 3 is done when every slot arrives
in Entra ID exactly as HR sent it.

23
00:01:22.625 --> 00:01:26.604
A sample check, V K sync oh
one, compares the slot values,

24
00:01:26.604 --> 00:01:29.950
and a reconciliation
report covers the rest.

25
00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:35.469
One key runs through all of it: an existing
enterprise ID the agency already issues,

26
00:01:35.469 --> 00:01:41.020
carried in the employee I D attribute,
which holds at most sixteen characters.

27
00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:48.670
Before touching the tree, measure
how each task is done today.

28
00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:50.632
The method is design guidance.

29
00:01:50.632 --> 00:01:54.820
Take real joiner, mover and leaver
events from the last ninety days,

30
00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:57.332
and follow each one through its records.

31
00:01:57.332 --> 00:02:00.745
For a joiner, that's the HR
action date, the ticket,

32
00:02:00.745 --> 00:02:03.983
the account and group
changes, and the device.

33
00:02:03.983 --> 00:02:06.818
For a mover, it's the
groups added and removed,

34
00:02:06.818 --> 00:02:09.732
and whether the old
access was ever reviewed.

35
00:02:09.732 --> 00:02:12.393
For a leaver, it's when
the account was disabled

36
00:02:12.393 --> 00:02:15.195
and when sessions and
credentials were revoked.

37
00:02:15.195 --> 00:02:19.995
And ask the right question: show me the
last one you did, not describe the process.

38
00:02:19.995 --> 00:02:22.207
Take every timestamp from a record.

39
00:02:22.207 --> 00:02:24.582
A step with no record is a finding.

40
00:02:24.582 --> 00:02:26.953
These numbers have to be traced by hand,

41
00:02:26.953 --> 00:02:31.030
because a ticket-driven estate
never records them as events.

42
00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:37.780
Four read-only sources give
the baseline its numbers.

43
00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:42.702
The Start Kit reads the forest: enabled
users, orphaned admin count accounts,

44
00:02:42.702 --> 00:02:46.218
empty groups, and the
delegations someone made by hand.

45
00:02:46.218 --> 00:02:51.755
The validation kit's four HR checks, run
against an HR export, give correlation

46
00:02:51.755 --> 00:02:57.293
coverage, empty coded slots, counts per
organization code, and duplicate keys.

47
00:02:57.293 --> 00:03:00.342
Run this early, expect
them to fail broadly.

48
00:03:00.342 --> 00:03:02.318
That failure is your baseline.

49
00:03:02.318 --> 00:03:05.429
The group-change audit gives
the mover and leaver trail.

50
00:03:05.429 --> 00:03:09.209
And joining the HR separation
date to the disable date on the

51
00:03:09.209 --> 00:03:13.518
employee I D gives stale access and
the separation-to-block interval.

52
00:03:13.518 --> 00:03:14.631
One trap here.

53
00:03:14.631 --> 00:03:20.242
Entra's audit log keeps thirty days with
P1 or P2, and it isn't retroactive.

54
00:03:20.242 --> 00:03:24.035
So a ninety-day trace leans
on HR, tickets and A D.

55
00:03:24.035 --> 00:03:26.200
Route the logs out now.

56
00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:34.163
Gate 0 starts with the Start Kit,
and the whole gate is read-only.

57
00:03:34.163 --> 00:03:37.913
Start at Tier A, a standard
account with no added rights.

58
00:03:37.913 --> 00:03:40.942
Every run first checks what
that account can read,

59
00:03:40.942 --> 00:03:43.750
and writes up the gaps
as a delegation request.

60
00:03:43.750 --> 00:03:47.225
Add Tier B, narrow and
time-bound read delegations,

61
00:03:47.225 --> 00:03:49.938
only where Tier A can't see what you need.

62
00:03:49.938 --> 00:03:54.413
Each run lands in a folder named
for its date, host and tier.

63
00:03:54.413 --> 00:03:58.958
A manifest hashes every file, so what
you commit is provably what ran,

64
00:03:58.958 --> 00:04:01.800
and a later run can be
compared file by file.

65
00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:07.044
Keep three things: every O U with its
size and its hand-made access entries,

66
00:04:07.044 --> 00:04:09.667
G P O links at domain, O U and site level,

67
00:04:09.667 --> 00:04:13.600
and every count marked not
collected rather than zero.

68
00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:16.124
And tell security
operations before you run,

69
00:04:16.124 --> 00:04:19.620
because broad enumeration
looks like reconnaissance.

70
00:04:23.370 --> 00:04:26.633
The rest of Gate 0 is a
decision record in Git.

71
00:04:26.633 --> 00:04:29.920
No check reads it, so the
record is the evidence.

72
00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:32.395
Every security group gets one decision.

73
00:04:32.395 --> 00:04:36.132
Keep it in A D if it only
guards on-premises resources.

74
00:04:36.132 --> 00:04:37.670
Sync it if it guards both.

75
00:04:37.670 --> 00:04:41.245
Make it cloud-only if it
guards only cloud resources.

76
00:04:41.245 --> 00:04:45.920
Convert it to dynamic only if its
membership really follows the attributes.

77
00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:47.570
Retire it if it's empty.

78
00:04:47.570 --> 00:04:49.632
Then map each O U function.

79
00:04:49.632 --> 00:04:53.207
Policy scope goes to
Conditional Access and Intune.

80
00:04:53.207 --> 00:04:58.494
Delegation goes to administrative units,
or to Azure role scopes for servers.

81
00:04:58.494 --> 00:05:03.083
And tag every O U and every
G P O half with its track.

82
00:05:03.083 --> 00:05:04.558
Record two traps.

83
00:05:04.558 --> 00:05:08.919
A site-linked G P O looks
unlinked to an O U-only search.

84
00:05:08.919 --> 00:05:11.282
And admin count overstates privilege.

85
00:05:11.282 --> 00:05:15.242
The service accounts the kit
flags go to the non-person track,

86
00:05:15.242 --> 00:05:17.697
and a beta Intune check, V K int oh seven,

87
00:05:17.697 --> 00:05:22.250
reads Group Policy analytics for
the computer half of the split.

88
00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:27.175
One honest slide.

89
00:05:27.175 --> 00:05:31.300
The overview simplified three
things, and at this depth they bite.

90
00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:32.913
This is design guidance.

91
00:05:32.913 --> 00:05:35.725
First, some O U's don't map cleanly.

92
00:05:35.725 --> 00:05:41.425
One may exist for a single hand-made
delegation, a G P O link, or a sync filter.

93
00:05:41.425 --> 00:05:44.313
Record those rather than
forcing them into the table.

94
00:05:44.313 --> 00:05:47.963
Second, HR codes aren't
always access boundaries.

95
00:05:47.963 --> 00:05:51.866
Convert a group only when its
membership really follows the codes,

96
00:05:51.866 --> 00:05:54.350
and give the misfits a reviewed exception.

97
00:05:54.350 --> 00:05:57.316
HR also enters actions
late or future-dated,

98
00:05:57.316 --> 00:06:01.188
and worker types go well beyond
employee and contractor.

99
00:06:01.188 --> 00:06:06.198
Third, removing the other writers can
be a project in itself: the help desk,

100
00:06:06.198 --> 00:06:11.125
self-service, Exchange's custom-attribute
commands, and O U write rights.

101
00:06:11.125 --> 00:06:14.175
Find every writer in the audit logs first.

102
00:06:14.175 --> 00:06:17.830
After Gate 2, any other
write counts as drift.

103
00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:27.018
Gate 1 fixes the slot register, the one
map of user slots the whole workbook uses.

104
00:06:27.018 --> 00:06:30.242
The employee I D attribute
carries the person key.

105
00:06:30.242 --> 00:06:34.969
Slots two and three carry the hire and
leave dates on the A D side, and a sync

106
00:06:34.969 --> 00:06:39.931
rule maps them to the native date
properties that Lifecycle Workflows read.

107
00:06:39.931 --> 00:06:45.330
Slot seven is account type, derived from
O U placement only during the transition.

108
00:06:45.330 --> 00:06:48.868
The three that drive access
are ten, eleven and twelve.

109
00:06:48.868 --> 00:06:53.505
That's the organization code, the worker
type code, and the position category.

110
00:06:53.505 --> 00:06:57.968
Rules can't read the native worker type,
which is why slot eleven mirrors it.

111
00:06:57.968 --> 00:07:03.856
Slot fifteen is Do Not Sync, read only by
a sync filter, never by an access rule.

112
00:07:03.856 --> 00:07:06.868
Slots one to three follow
Microsoft's worksheet.

113
00:07:06.868 --> 00:07:10.290
The rest are the workbook's
proposals, to confirm.

114
00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:19.577
The rest of Gate 1 comes down to three
rules: one store, one writer, one owner.

115
00:07:19.577 --> 00:07:22.591
The data architect picks
the store, in order.

116
00:07:22.591 --> 00:07:27.655
Native properties come first, then extension
attributes for anything a rule reads,

117
00:07:27.655 --> 00:07:30.665
then directory extensions
if you need more slots.

118
00:07:30.665 --> 00:07:35.152
Custom security attributes hold
sensitive values that no rule needs.

119
00:07:35.152 --> 00:07:39.465
The provisioning service becomes the
only writer of every schema field.

120
00:07:39.465 --> 00:07:42.514
Every other path, the
help desk, self-service,

121
00:07:42.514 --> 00:07:47.002
and Exchange administration, goes
on a list for removal at Gate 2.

122
00:07:47.002 --> 00:07:49.404
Put codes, never display names,

123
00:07:49.404 --> 00:07:53.341
in anything a rule reads,
and HR owns the code lists.

124
00:07:53.341 --> 00:07:57.379
To validate, run the two
checks on the key's carrier,

125
00:07:57.379 --> 00:08:00.827
V K H R oh one and oh
four, and expect findings.

126
00:08:00.827 --> 00:08:02.327
Sign the register early.

127
00:08:02.327 --> 00:08:07.740
Once a rule reads a slot, changing it
means rewriting every rule that reads it.

128
00:08:11.490 --> 00:08:15.504
Gate 2 proves the attributes,
starting with the kit's HR checks,

129
00:08:15.504 --> 00:08:18.102
first on the pilot and then on the estate.

130
00:08:18.102 --> 00:08:23.290
Run the validation kit with the HR and
dynamic group tracks and an input folder.

131
00:08:23.290 --> 00:08:26.715
It signs in with only the read
scopes those checks need.

132
00:08:26.715 --> 00:08:29.340
At this gate, read the HR rows.

133
00:08:29.340 --> 00:08:31.640
The input folder holds two files.

134
00:08:31.640 --> 00:08:34.093
The exceptions file lists accounts that

135
00:08:34.093 --> 00:08:36.690
legitimately have no
key, each with an owner.

136
00:08:36.690 --> 00:08:39.203
An exception with no owner doesn't count.

137
00:08:39.203 --> 00:08:43.115
The HR export gives the worker
count per organization code.

138
00:08:43.115 --> 00:08:47.740
Without it, the reconciliation check
reports not applicable, never pass.

139
00:08:47.740 --> 00:08:52.665
Each check has one failure condition:
an enabled account with no key,

140
00:08:52.665 --> 00:08:57.415
an empty coded slot, a count that
differs from HR, or a key held twice.

141
00:08:57.415 --> 00:09:00.890
Fix each one at the
source, never in the copy.

142
00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:09.065
Here's what a failing check looks like,
using the kit's own fictional fixture.

143
00:09:09.065 --> 00:09:12.265
V K H R oh one fails
with a count of three.

144
00:09:12.265 --> 00:09:13.765
One row is the trap.

145
00:09:13.765 --> 00:09:17.244
That account is in the exceptions
file, but with no owner,

146
00:09:17.244 --> 00:09:19.815
so the check still
reports it, and says why.

147
00:09:19.815 --> 00:09:23.277
The reconciliation check
shows each organization code.

148
00:09:23.277 --> 00:09:24.553
One code agrees.

149
00:09:24.553 --> 00:09:27.440
One has fewer users in Entra than in HR.

150
00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:31.265
One exists in Entra but not
in HR, so someone typed it.

151
00:09:31.265 --> 00:09:33.953
And one is in HR but missing from Entra.

152
00:09:33.953 --> 00:09:37.003
Every row is fixed at
HR, not the directory.

153
00:09:37.003 --> 00:09:40.391
The duplicate check finds
one key held by two users.

154
00:09:40.391 --> 00:09:42.240
Fail means at least one finding.

155
00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:45.102
Not applicable and error are never a pass.

156
00:09:45.102 --> 00:09:48.752
Everything lands in a run
folder with a hashed manifest,

157
00:09:48.752 --> 00:09:51.400
and that folder is Gate 2's evidence.

158
00:09:55.150 --> 00:09:58.725
Three more steps close Gate
2, each with its own owner.

159
00:09:58.725 --> 00:10:03.575
The authorizing official sets the correlation
threshold through risk governance.

160
00:10:03.575 --> 00:10:07.387
Ninety-nine percent of the pilot
is a proposed starting point,

161
00:10:07.387 --> 00:10:10.825
not a rule, and a risk-tiered
threshold is usually better.

162
00:10:10.825 --> 00:10:13.548
Every enabled account is either correlated

163
00:10:13.548 --> 00:10:16.050
or on a dated exception
list with an owner.

164
00:10:16.050 --> 00:10:20.548
Identity engineering removes the
other writers: O U write rights and

165
00:10:20.548 --> 00:10:25.362
Exchange's commands in A D, and the help
desk and self-service paths in Entra.

166
00:10:25.362 --> 00:10:27.808
Security operations routes the sign-in,

167
00:10:27.808 --> 00:10:31.737
audit and provisioning logs out
before the pilot's first change.

168
00:10:31.737 --> 00:10:34.674
In the running example,
a second account turns

169
00:10:34.674 --> 00:10:37.688
up holding the analyst's
key, an old test account.

170
00:10:37.688 --> 00:10:42.575
HR confirms the real record, and the
test account is disabled and listed.

171
00:10:42.575 --> 00:10:43.763
It isn't deleted.

172
00:10:43.763 --> 00:10:46.380
The C I S O owns this gate.

173
00:10:50.130 --> 00:10:54.780
Gate 3 maps the attributes through
sync, and its failures are quiet.

174
00:10:54.780 --> 00:10:56.405
There are two landing zones.

175
00:10:56.405 --> 00:11:00.746
For most users, HR writes into A
D through the provisioning agent,

176
00:11:00.746 --> 00:11:02.793
and sync carries the values up.

177
00:11:02.793 --> 00:11:06.605
For cloud-mastered users,
HR writes Entra directly.

178
00:11:06.605 --> 00:11:08.382
Use the same carrier in both,

179
00:11:08.382 --> 00:11:11.705
so a rule never has to know
which zone a user came from.

180
00:11:11.705 --> 00:11:15.398
Use Cloud Sync unless you need
something only Connect Sync does,

181
00:11:15.398 --> 00:11:17.418
and password hash sync everywhere.

182
00:11:17.418 --> 00:11:18.879
Then map the slots.

183
00:11:18.879 --> 00:11:22.960
The two date slots map to the
native hire and leave dates,

184
00:11:22.960 --> 00:11:25.355
in the format A D strings require.

185
00:11:25.355 --> 00:11:29.417
The transitional rule derives
account type from O U placement.

186
00:11:29.417 --> 00:11:33.217
It's a bridge, not an end
state, and Gate 8 removes it.

187
00:11:33.217 --> 00:11:36.510
And the Do Not Sync filter
keys on slot fifteen.

188
00:11:40.260 --> 00:11:43.876
This is the record HR sends
to the provisioning endpoint,

189
00:11:43.876 --> 00:11:46.723
one operation from the
example code, trimmed.

190
00:11:46.723 --> 00:11:49.648
Each request holds at
most fifty operations.

191
00:11:49.648 --> 00:11:52.451
The external I D carries
the person number,

192
00:11:52.451 --> 00:11:55.173
and it matches the employee I D attribute.

193
00:11:55.173 --> 00:11:57.873
If nothing matches, a user is created.

194
00:11:57.873 --> 00:12:02.998
The enterprise extension carries department,
division, cost center and manager.

195
00:12:02.998 --> 00:12:06.223
The agency's own extension
carries the codes

196
00:12:06.223 --> 00:12:09.273
that land in the slots,
plus the dates in U T C.

197
00:12:09.273 --> 00:12:14.472
The service answers two oh two accepted,
with a link to the provisioning logs.

198
00:12:14.472 --> 00:12:17.173
Updates are just the same
record posted again.

199
00:12:17.173 --> 00:12:21.026
And the rule that matters most:
send the whole record every time,

200
00:12:21.026 --> 00:12:24.435
because a missing attribute
can clear the target value.

201
00:12:24.435 --> 00:12:27.022
The example client dry-runs by default.

202
00:12:27.022 --> 00:12:30.550
Send five to ten records
before the full extract.

203
00:12:34.300 --> 00:12:35.988
Two Gate 3 traps.

204
00:12:35.988 --> 00:12:37.388
The first is matching.

205
00:12:37.388 --> 00:12:41.153
When sync meets an object that
already exists in the cloud,

206
00:12:41.153 --> 00:12:45.388
a hard match on the source anchor
makes A D the source of authority.

207
00:12:45.388 --> 00:12:50.536
Failing that, a soft match on the user
principal name and the email address applies,

208
00:12:50.536 --> 00:12:53.337
but only if the cloud
object has no anchor yet.

209
00:12:53.337 --> 00:12:58.499
Either one overwrites the cloud values
with A D's, the password hash included.

210
00:12:58.499 --> 00:13:03.075
So clean A D first, and keep both
matches blocked outside a planned window.

211
00:13:03.075 --> 00:13:04.925
The second trap is a date.

212
00:13:04.925 --> 00:13:09.757
By April seventh, twenty twenty-seven,
every Connect Sync server needs version

213
00:13:09.757 --> 00:13:14.737
two point six point eighty-four or later,
plus application-based authentication.

214
00:13:14.737 --> 00:13:16.350
Otherwise sync stops.

215
00:13:16.350 --> 00:13:18.412
The upgrade alone isn't enough.

216
00:13:18.412 --> 00:13:20.225
You also have to run the wizard.

217
00:13:20.225 --> 00:13:23.650
And never run two active
Connect Sync servers.

218
00:13:27.400 --> 00:13:31.300
Gate 3's proof starts with a
kit check, V K sync oh one.

219
00:13:31.300 --> 00:13:36.572
Export a sample of users from A D, with
the employee I D and the three coded slots,

220
00:13:36.572 --> 00:13:40.937
and the check compares those values
with Entra, joined on the key.

221
00:13:40.937 --> 00:13:43.914
It flags a sample row with no Entra user,

222
00:13:43.914 --> 00:13:47.076
a key held twice, and
any slot that differs.

223
00:13:47.076 --> 00:13:49.986
It reads values, not
the sync configuration,

224
00:13:49.986 --> 00:13:53.463
so the reconciliation report
still covers the rest.

225
00:13:53.463 --> 00:13:58.587
That means counts within one percent,
no sync errors, a full sync reconciled

226
00:13:58.587 --> 00:14:04.125
before deltas, shadow-mode differences
explained, and zero unmatched creations.

227
00:14:04.125 --> 00:14:05.753
In the running example,

228
00:14:05.753 --> 00:14:09.950
the analyst's codes land in A D
and arrive in Entra unchanged.

229
00:14:09.950 --> 00:14:15.250
To back out, turn the scoping filter back
and let the next cycle remove the values.

230
00:14:15.250 --> 00:14:19.588
Never delete synced users in Entra,
because sync just restores them.

231
00:14:19.588 --> 00:14:21.224
Pause the provisioning job.

232
00:14:21.224 --> 00:14:24.910
Don't restart it, because
restart purges what's waiting.

233
00:14:28.660 --> 00:14:32.972
To close, here's what each gate
leaves in Git, and what comes next.

234
00:14:32.972 --> 00:14:37.722
Gate 0 leaves the Start Kit run, the
group decisions and the unit map.

235
00:14:37.722 --> 00:14:42.773
Gate 1 leaves the signed slot register,
which every rule and persona will read.

236
00:14:42.773 --> 00:14:46.296
Gate 2 leaves the validation
run folder, the threshold,

237
00:14:46.296 --> 00:14:49.272
the owned exceptions,
and the removed writers.

238
00:14:49.272 --> 00:14:53.398
Gate 3 leaves the sample check's
result, the reconciliation report,

239
00:14:53.398 --> 00:14:56.248
and live pilot data for
the shadow comparison.

240
00:14:56.248 --> 00:14:59.047
The attributes are now true and flowing.

241
00:14:59.047 --> 00:15:04.300
Part 2 takes Gates 4 to 6: the dynamic
group library and administrative units,

242
00:15:04.300 --> 00:15:09.636
the Conditional Access personas and
targeting conventions, and P I V binding.

243
00:15:09.636 --> 00:15:12.447
Those are the mechanisms
that read the attributes.

244
00:15:12.447 --> 00:15:13.270
Thank you.
